C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not gather what churches say about the oracles against the nations and leave the reader to choose. It says what the author believes the prophets themselves teach about the nations they judged, it gives the evidence from their own sentences, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ words, so that a reader who disagrees knows exactly where."),
("p", "The question is narrow, and the prophets raise it. Jeremiah is told at his calling, “I have this day set thee over the nations and over the kingdoms, to root out, and to pull down, and to destroy, and to throw down, to build, and to plant” (Jeremiah 1:10). Amos opens his book with a roar from Zion and six sentences, each beginning “For three transgressions,” against Damascus, Gaza, Tyrus, Edom, Ammon and Moab (Amos 1:3–2:3). Isaiah has a “burden” for Babylon, Moab, Damascus, Egypt and Tyre (Isaiah 13:1; 15:1; 17:1; 19:1; 23:1). Ezekiel and Jeremiah lay out whole chapters of them. Readers have taken these oracles in three ways. Some say they were all fulfilled long ago and are now history. Some say they are all pictures of the church and its enemies. Some say they are all still to come. The volume asks a question that the three answers skip: which of these oracles does Scripture itself report as fulfilled, which does it hold open, and what does each say it rests on?"),
("p", "The position is this. The oracles against the nations are not one prophecy on one timeline. Each carries its own ground, which is what that nation did; its own term, which is how long or how soon; and its own ending, which is what the text says becomes of it. The text sorts them into three classes. In the closed class the sentence is carried out and no restoration follows. In the part-fulfilled class the text itself reports what happened and what remains. In the open class a clause of restoration is attached that no later passage reports as fulfilled, and the volume holds those clauses open: the nations are blessed through Israel’s restored line in the Kingdom, and not through the Body of Christ or through the church."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passage, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for a position the author has read in a free text, and where an objection is stated, it is stated as the objector would state it and then answered from the verses."),
("p", "The volume does not take up what its neighbours argue. Babylon, with Revelation 17 and 18, is read in Prophetic Commentary Volume 3, and Edom, with Isaiah 63, in Prophetic Commentary Volume 5; this volume cites them and does not repeat them. The conditional word of Jeremiah 18:7–10 is argued in the first volume of this series, and the sins of Israel’s worship in the second. The day of the LORD is read in Study 7, the court of the living nations of Matthew 25 in Prophecy & Mystery Volume 5, and the Abrahamic promise of Genesis 12:3 in Covenantal Volume 1. The volume also leaves aside the passages sometimes taken to speak of an unseen being behind the kings of Babylon and Tyre (Isaiah 14:12–15; Ezekiel 28:11–19). It reads what each prophet says to the king he addresses, and names no further figure. Finally, it builds no doctrine for the Body of Christ from the prophets’ oracles. They were spoken to nations about nations, with Israel at the centre, and the volume keeps them there."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "The oracles against the nations are not one prophecy on one timeline. Each names its ground, its term and its ending, and Scripture itself reports some as closed, some as part-fulfilled and some as open."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "Every oracle names a ground (what the nation did) and a verdict (what the LORD will do), and the volume tests each by four questions: against whom, for what, for how long, and what ending.", "Chapters 1 and 2"),
    ("2", "A closed oracle is one whose sentence the text carries out or the passage itself reports, and which names no restoration; Nineveh is the clearest case.", "Chapters 3 and 4"),
    ("3", "A part-fulfilled oracle is one in which the text itself says what has happened and what has not; Ezekiel 29:17–20 reports that the siege of Tyre paid no wages and gives Egypt to Nebuchadrezzar in its place.", "Chapters 5, 6 and 7"),
    ("4", "An open oracle is one with a restoration clause that no later passage reports fulfilled (Egypt, Moab, Ammon, Elam, Tyre); the volume holds these open as nations blessed through Israel’s restored line in the Kingdom, not as the Body and not as the church.", "Chapters 4 to 8"),
  ], [0.12, 0.62, 0.26]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any oracle against a nation and ask four questions of it in order: against whom is it spoken, what is the ground, what is the term, and what ending does the text give? If every oracle ends in the same way, or if every oracle carries the same ground, the thesis fails. If the oracles instead differ, and the differences are in the text and not in the reader’s theory, the thesis stands, and the reader will see why the volume refuses to read all of them as fulfilled, all of them as pictures, or all of them as future."),
]
